        <span>/</span>
        <span aria-current="page">Solo Travel Statistics 2026</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="stats-hero-title">Solo travel statistics 2026</h1>
      <p class="stats-hero-subtitle">Key facts, trends, and data on solo travel, backpacker demographics, and travel companion apps.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="stats-content">
    <div class="stats-content-inner">

      <p class="stats-intro">Solo travel is one of the fastest-growing segments in global tourism. The solo travel market was valued at approximately <strong>USD 550 billion in 2025</strong> and is projected to grow at a compound annual growth rate of 13&ndash;15% through 2033. Solo travellers now account for roughly <strong>18% of global tourism bookings</strong>, with 59% of travellers reporting they have taken a solo trip in the past five years. Women represent the majority of solo travellers at 60&ndash;70%, and safety and loneliness remain the top concerns, yet 78% of solo travellers report gains in confidence and self-discovery.</p>

      <h2>Solo travel growth</h2>

      <p>Solo travel has moved from niche to mainstream. Industry data shows consistent acceleration across bookings, searches, and market size.</p>

      <div class="stats-grid">
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">42%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">increase in solo travel bookings over the past two years <sup><a href="https://www.condorferries.co.uk/solo-travel-statistics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Condor Ferries</a></sup></span>
          <span class="stat-source">Condor Ferries / Hostelworld, 2025</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">59%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">of travellers have taken a solo trip in the past five years, up from 46% in 2025 <sup><a href="https://www.travelboommarketing.com/blog/travelboom-2026-leisure-travel-study-reveals-major-shifts-in-travel-behavior-of-american-consumers" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TravelBoom</a></sup></span>
          <span class="stat-source">TravelBoom Leisure Travel Study, 2026</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">72%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">increase in Google searches for "solo travel" from 2023 to 2024</span>
          <span class="stat-source">Google Trends data</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">$550B</span>
          <span class="stat-label">estimated global solo travel market size in 2025 <sup><a href="https://www.sphericalinsights.com/our-insights/solo-travel-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spherical Insights</a></sup></span>
          <span class="stat-source">Spherical Insights market report</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ul>
        <li>The solo travel market is projected to reach <strong>USD 1.5&ndash;2.1 trillion by 2033&ndash;2035</strong>, depending on the source, growing at a CAGR of 13&ndash;15%. <sup><a href="https://www.grandviewresearch.com/press-release/global-solo-travel-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Grand View Research</a></sup> <sup><a href="https://www.custommarketinsights.com/press-releases/solo-travel-market-size/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Custom Market Insights</a></sup></li>
        <li>Solo travel now represents approximately <strong>11% of all global travel</strong> by market share. <sup><a href="https://www.sphericalinsights.com/our-insights/solo-travel-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spherical Insights</a></sup></li>
        <li>Millennials account for <strong>52% of solo trips</strong>, with Gen Z and Millennial travellers leading search interest: 76% plan to take a solo trip. <sup><a href="https://www.condorferries.co.uk/solo-travel-statistics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Condor Ferries</a></sup></li>
        <li>Searches for single-occupancy hotel rooms surged by <strong>170%</strong> on major booking platforms.</li>
        <li>The Asia Pacific solo travel market is growing fastest, projected at a <strong>14.9% CAGR from 2025 to 2035</strong>. <sup><a href="https://www.custommarketinsights.com/press-releases/solo-travel-market-size/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Custom Market Insights</a></sup></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Solo traveller demographics</h2>

      <p>The solo travel demographic is broader than the backpacker stereotype suggests. It spans every age group, skews female, and includes a growing number of first-time travellers.</p>

      <div class="stats-grid">
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">60&ndash;70%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">of solo travellers are women <sup><a href="https://www.hostelworld.com/state-of-solo-travel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hostelworld</a></sup> <sup><a href="https://www.condorferries.co.uk/solo-travel-statistics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Condor Ferries</a></sup></span>
          <span class="stat-source">Hostelworld / Condor Ferries, 2025</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">25&ndash;34</span>
          <span class="stat-label">most common age range for solo backpackers (Hostelworld data: 66% are 18&ndash;30) <sup><a href="https://www.hostelworld.com/state-of-solo-travel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hostelworld</a></sup></span>
          <span class="stat-source">Hostelworld State of Solo Travel, 2025</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">63%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">of first-time solo travellers plan to travel solo again <sup><a href="https://www.travelboommarketing.com/blog/travelboom-2026-leisure-travel-study-reveals-major-shifts-in-travel-behavior-of-american-consumers" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TravelBoom</a></sup></span>
          <span class="stat-source">TravelBoom, 2026</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">18%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">of solo travellers identify as LGBTQIA+</span>
          <span class="stat-source">Solo travel industry surveys</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ul>
        <li>The average age of all solo travellers (not just backpackers) is <strong>47 years old</strong>, reflecting strong participation from Gen X and Boomers alongside younger travellers.</li>
        <li>Among hostel-staying backpackers, the demographic skews younger: <strong>66% are aged 18&ndash;30</strong>, with 39% aged 18&ndash;24.</li>
        <li>Most people first travel solo <strong>between the ages of 22 and 30</strong>.</li>
        <li>Solo travel is popular globally, with particularly high participation in <strong>Scandinavia, the Netherlands, Germany, the UK, Australia, and the United States</strong>. In Japan, solo travel is a cultural movement known as <em>ohitorisama</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>72% of American women</strong> have taken a solo trip, the highest rate globally (Booking.com survey, 2014).</li>
        <li>Of those who travelled solo in 2024, <strong>36% planned four to five additional solo trips</strong> the following year, indicating high repeat rates.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Safety and social concerns</h2>

      <p>Despite the growth in solo travel, safety and loneliness remain the most commonly cited barriers, particularly for women considering their first solo trip.</p>

      <div class="stats-grid">
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">62%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">cite personal safety as their primary concern when considering solo travel <sup><a href="https://talkerresearch.com/82-of-americans-have-traveled-solo-or-want-to-but-many-are-hesitant/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Talker Research</a></sup></span>
          <span class="stat-source">Road Scholar / Talker Research survey, 2026</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">57%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">estimated to express fear of loneliness and isolation as a major concern</span>
          <span class="stat-source">Industry surveys (secondary aggregators), 2025&ndash;2026</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">44%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">of solo travellers estimated to experience loneliness at some point during their trip</span>
          <span class="stat-source">Solo travel behaviour studies (secondary aggregators)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">71%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">of solo travellers actively seek social connection on the road <sup><a href="https://www.hostelworld.com/state-of-solo-travel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hostelworld</a></sup></span>
          <span class="stat-source">Hostelworld, 2025</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>63% of women</strong> cite safety as their main barrier to solo travel, compared to 18% of men, a significant gender gap. <sup><a href="https://talkerresearch.com/82-of-americans-have-traveled-solo-or-want-to-but-many-are-hesitant/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Talker Research</a></sup></li>
        <li>The top safety anxieties are <strong>walking at night (59%), staying somewhere unfamiliar (51%), and navigating public transport (44%)</strong>. <sup><a href="https://talkerresearch.com/82-of-americans-have-traveled-solo-or-want-to-but-many-are-hesitant/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Talker Research</a></sup></li>
        <li><strong>38% of solo travellers</strong> have avoided entire countries due to safety concerns.</li>
        <li>The most common way solo travellers address safety is by <strong>sharing their location with friends and family</strong> (30%).</li>
        <li>Surveys suggest that <strong>68% of solo travellers form meaningful friendships</strong> during their trips, and 58% say meeting new people is a primary motivation, up from 43% in 2024. <sup><a href="https://www.hostelworld.com/state-of-solo-travel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hostelworld</a></sup></li>
        <li>The most common ways solo travellers meet others include: <strong>hostels and common rooms, organised tours and activities, travel companion apps, online travel communities</strong>, and language exchange meetups.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Budget and spending</h2>

      <p>Daily costs vary dramatically by region. Budget-conscious backpackers can stretch their money furthest in Southeast Asia, while Western Europe demands significantly more.</p>

      <div class="stats-table-wrap">
        <table class="stats-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Region</th>
              <th>Daily budget (backpacker)</th>
              <th>Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Southeast Asia</td>
              <td>$20&ndash;50</td>
              <td>Vietnam cheapest ($20&ndash;35), Thailand higher ($25&ndash;50), islands cost more</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>South America</td>
              <td>$30&ndash;45</td>
              <td>Bolivia cheapest, Colombia/Brazil higher; $1,000&ndash;1,300/month typical</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Eastern Europe / Balkans</td>
              <td>$30&ndash;45</td>
              <td>Albania, Kosovo, Serbia, Poland, Hungary offer best value</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Western Europe</td>
              <td>$80&ndash;150+</td>
              <td>Scandinavia 3x Balkans cost; shoulder season saves 20&ndash;30%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Central America</td>
              <td>$35&ndash;55</td>
              <td>Guatemala and Honduras cheapest, Costa Rica higher</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="stats-table-note">Figures cover accommodation, food, local transport, and basic activities. Excludes international flights, visas, and insurance.</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Solo travellers pay an estimated <strong>47% more on average</strong> than couples, largely due to single supplement charges on accommodation.</li>
        <li>Sharing accommodation with a travel companion can reduce daily costs by <strong>20&ndash;40%</strong>, making it one of the most effective budget strategies.</li>
        <li>Travel industry reports suggest the majority of solo backpackers travel on a <strong>budget or mid-range budget</strong>. Long-term backpackers typically spend between <strong>$800 and $2,200 per month</strong> depending on region.</li>
        <li>The average cost of a one-year backpacking trip is estimated at approximately <strong>$20,000</strong>, though this varies widely by destination mix.</li>
        <li>Shoulder season travel (e.g., September in Europe, May in Southeast Asia) saves <strong>20&ndash;30% on accommodation and flights</strong>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Travel companion app usage</h2>

      <p>As solo travel has grown, so has demand for platforms that help travellers find compatible companions. Travel companion apps address the two biggest solo travel pain points: loneliness and cost.</p>

      <div class="stats-grid">
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">71%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">of solo travellers actively seek connection while travelling <sup><a href="https://www.hostelworld.com/state-of-solo-travel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hostelworld</a></sup></span>
          <span class="stat-source">Hostelworld, 2025</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">68%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">form meaningful friendships during solo trips</span>
          <span class="stat-source">Solo travel behaviour studies</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">78%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">of women say they would travel solo if they could join organised groups or find verified companions</span>
          <span class="stat-source">Solo Female Travellers survey, 2024</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-number">42%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">increase in solo travel bookings, driving demand for companion-matching tools <sup><a href="https://www.condorferries.co.uk/solo-travel-statistics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Condor Ferries</a></sup></span>
          <span class="stat-source">Condor Ferries / Hostelworld, 2024&ndash;2026</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ul>
        <li>Travel companion apps have emerged as a distinct category alongside hostel apps and group tour platforms. The most commonly searched queries include <strong>"find a travel buddy," "travel companion app," "solo travel meetup,"</strong> and <strong>"travel partner finder."</strong></li>
        <li>Research indicates that the desire for social connection among solo travellers is increasing: <strong>58% say meeting new people is a primary travel motivation</strong>, up from 43% in 2024.</li>
        <li>The gender-safety gap is a major driver of app adoption. Surveys suggest that <strong>78% of women</strong> who hesitate to travel solo would do so if they could find organised groups or verified companions (Solo Female Travellers survey, 2024).</li>
        <li>Travel companion platforms use a variety of matching approaches: destination overlap, travel style compatibility, budget alignment, verified profiles, and in-app messaging.</li>
      </ul>

